A||||13||K9||Temp. Gage Problem
Z||000000||K9||09-01-99||11:33 PM||||I have a 85 GT and the temp gage dosen't work properley.I have changed to sender unit and traced all the wireing but the problem still remaines. The only thing I can think of is the gage. The gage come on during the bulb and chekout to be o.k. I have heard that changeing the gage will not help as this is a wireing problem from GM.Can anyone help. Should i spend about $200 on a new gage. What if it still dosent work?
Z||000001||cmyokley||09-02-99||05:43 AM||||I would not spend $200 for a new gage. My temp. gage was not working either (after tracking all wiring and replacing the sender) so several weeks ago I spent $14.95 at a junk yard for a complete gage cluster, swapped parts around and now have a working temp gage.<p>Just as a thought you do know that there are two temp switches? One for the idot light and one for the temp gage. Location of the switches depends on engine type.<p>Good luck.
Z||000002||batboy||09-02-99||06:34 AM||||The hot light and the temp gauge both run off the same temp sending unit. There are two wires that plug into this sending unit, one goes to the temp gauge and the other goes to the hot light.<p>K9, exactly what does your temp gauge do, or don't do. When the engine first cranks over as you are starting it, does the temp gauge needle "peg" then settle back down to the 100 degree mark after it starts and runs? That's what it's supposed to do anyway.
Z||000003||K9||09-02-99||02:48 PM||||batboy- The gage "Pags" (goes all the way to hot)when I start the car then goes back to the cold mark. As the engine gets hot the gage tries to move but cant???
Z||000004||batboy||09-02-99||04:39 PM||||Weird! Well, the gauge pegs, so the sending unit and wires are probably OK. Sounds like maybe it needs a new gauge. The gas gauge and the temp gauge are part of a separate unit within the main instrument cluster. I've seen these in every other salvage yard that have Fieros. I actually have an extra, but I want to keep it, sorry. I'm pretty sure Kick Hill has them and Ed Parks probably has an extra one or two also.
Z||000005||theogre||09-02-99||05:01 PM||||the gauge pegging is caused by the improperly wired gauge feed, it has nothing to do with the sender. The sender is likely gone.
Z||000006||batboy||09-02-99||06:35 PM||||On second thought, Ogre's right about the pegging at start-up not meaning the sender is OK. That actually means the gauge is working at least some what working. Also, it does mean the wiring is OK. You might try a new sending unit, they're easy to replace.
Z||000007||K9||09-03-99||05:36 AM||||I gess I forget to say that I have replaced the sending unit with a new one but the problem still remaines. Any other ideas would help and thankyou for all the responce. It is good to know that so-many people shair a common interest and are willing to help. 
Z||000008||DJRice||09-03-99||06:00 AM||||Is it possible that you have replaced the wrong temp sensor?  There are two; One for ECM and one for the gauge.  The easy way to test the wiring is to remove the wire from the sensor and ground it.  Your gauge should 'peg' at 260 deg.<p>BTW I believe the sensor you should be looking at is the one to the right of the Thermo Housing. 
Z||000009||toopak||09-03-99||09:46 AM||||Heres an alternative for you. My gauge worked but was not accurate, indicating on the cold side. Replaced the sender but no help. Bought a old fashioned mech. temp gauge & brkt for 20$ and mounted on my right side buttress so can turn my head ,look thru window and ck temp. This gauge is also very accurate. I think the stock gauge combo is priced at 85$ with temp and gas together.
Z||000010||Speadraser||09-03-99||10:25 AM||||If your needle pegs then the gauge is working.  It doesn't mean that the wiring or sender is okay.  I had a problem that was similar to this and after some monkeying around I figured out some things.  Your sender is located near the #5 sparkplug next to the engine lifting hook and tach filter.  My sender doesn't have a plastic connector.  I think the heat from the header melted it.  Anyway my temp gauge would peg-out and get physically stuck on some part in the gauge.  Well eventually the needle fell off the shaft.  Since the needle was off I couldn't remember where the needle started when it was cold so I could put the needle back on the shaft.  Somewhere I found out what resistance the sender is at max temp.  I hooked up a series of resistors at the sender then carefully placed the needle back on the shaft.  Here is where you should pay attention...  I then removed my resistor contraption and replaced the wires.  I didn't pay attention which terminal they went on.  Later I found my gauge not registering.  I went back to my schematics and found that the two terminals are for two different circuits(read batboy's post).  I switched the wires and bada-bing bada-boom. the temp gauge worked.  If your interested in more information like the exact resistance the sender is at cold and hot temps email me and I'll look them up and post them here.  I wouldn't replace the sender it's accuracy isn't crucial, but it needs to work!  Using a multimeter to check continuity in a circuit should be the first thing you check before replacing anything whether its a sender, sensor or headlight.<br>Speady
Z||000011||K9||09-03-99||02:54 PM||||I have checked the wires and grounded the green wire to check the gage. It checks out o.k. Speadraser,you said somthing about making sure that the two wires at the sender are plugedin the right order. My connecter has also melted off. How do I know if my wires are in the right order,At the sending unit.  
Z||000012||Andy||09-03-99||05:45 PM||||About a month a go I had problems with my temp gauge ,too. Would get stuck way past 260 and stay there.I replaced the sender and everything checked out,Done the grounding trick,everything worked like it was suppose to.A day later it was stuck again.I found a used gauge cluster at a junk yard and was going to repalce the gauge it self.But before I relaced it, I rewired the the system.I haven't had any more problems since then and still have a gauge incase I need it.It took me a couple of hours and didn't cost nothing.<br>You can find a tempgauge rewiring diagram on<br>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/tempgauge/tempgauge.htm       thanks to Greendano who shared this info with us a couple a month ago.Thanks to theogre who descibed on doing the same,but without cutting wires.<br>I sure would try to rewire it first and see if it fixes the problem
Z||000013||MCordray||09-04-99||05:14 PM||||Just some insight... About 8 months ago, my temp needle would get stuck way past the 260 degree mark, right when I cranked the engine. After smacking on the dash cluster a couple of times, it would move back into its normal position. What was happening was that the needle was getting caught on the metal lip of the gage opening. I ended up taking the cluster apart, inserting some black electrical tape between the metal opening and gauge backdrop. I've never had a sticky temp gauge needle since. 
